{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for the log contract amendment at 993e95d99e3645794e966b93abf969ffb1341db3, resting on all six artifacts, with docs/protocol.md first. Byte-based segment closing, signed layout selection, migration and loud refusal are useful, but three gaps prevent this design from completing the requested full scope safely.\n\n[P2] Define a recoverable encoding for oversized entries already sealed before the upgrade. R-LOG-18 constrains only entries sealed after deployment. A legal older entry after the last confirmed checkpoint can exceed B, even if its size is below Artifacts' 32 MiB limit. R-LOG-16 places it at or after from, and R-LOG-17's reference function emits an oversized one-entry segment. R-LOG-19 then refuses every future cohort containing it; retrying with a new N cannot remove that immutable entry. I ran the actual segmentStarts example: a 10-byte confirmed entry followed by an older 8,388,609-byte unpublished line, with layout.from=1, produces starts [0,1] and a new segment larger than B. Exact-bound, next-byte, 1,000-entry and already-published large-prefix controls also pass. Specify chunking or another authenticated bounded representation that preserves the original entry bytes/hash/signature, including old replay contexts and policy blobs that have not yet been published. Add a migration acceptance case with an unpublished old oversized line; do not solve it by discarding the sealed entry or leaving that room's log permanently unpublishable.\n\n[P2] A refused retry is not a completion proof for all prior unknown attempts. R-LOG-20 reads the parent after a definite refusal and then discards the cohort. The refusal proves only that this request did not apply. An earlier attempt can have lost its answer while still executing remotely. Sequence: push C is unknown; a retry of C is definitively refused; readback is parent P; the Room drops C and chooses another cohort/layout; the original push later changes the ref to C. The Room has discarded the identity needed to reconcile its own earlier write and now sees an unexpected writer. Keep unresolved attempts/cohort identity durable until they are resolved, or require and describe a provider completion/cancellation proof covering all earlier writes. Readback at P, expiry and revocation are not that proof. The publisher's before-any-send guard is different: it can retire a cohort only if that cohort has no earlier unknown effects. Add this delayed-earlier-push/refused-retry case, including refusal during migration. This is a counterexample to the proposed protocol, not a claim that an unimplemented amendment already fails a runtime test.\n\n[P2] Keep mandatory functionality at scale instead of suppressing it to fit new object sizes. R-LOG-18 ends notify evaluation without its usual notified record, opens no required revert lane, refuses landing after oversized recomputation, and leaves other system work undefined once counts become large. Saying counts are ones no room reaches is incompatible with this project's full-scope scaling direction. A post-push policy-activated event particularly needs a precise safe boundary: the canonical change may already have landed when recording its activation is found too large. An operational admin item alone is not a signed replayable substitute for the event that explains the state. The new 1 MiB policy/entry ceilings and 256-checker limit should not become arbitrary functional limits merely because a Git object has a byte limit. Preserve logical results with bounded/chunked collections and authenticated references, or justify any independently adopted semantic limit with a safe, complete failure protocol. R-LOG-19's single fan-out level also explicitly stops publication after enough retained objects share a prefix; its segments tree remains flat. Specify deterministic additional fan-out/sharding so these metadata trees stay bounded as work continues. Keep the guard as protection, while defining how the room can recover and continue. Add large notify/revert/configuration-activation/retained-prefix acceptance cases that still perform and record their required work.\n\nArchitecture: signed Checkpoint.layout and immutable from are clear, preserve entry hashes, and make boundaries reproducible. New count/byte boundaries and migration of retained paths can fit the current EntrySource/index publisher. The lane-edit list, explicit verifier failures and ordering verifier/decoder/classifier before writers are good. Objects inherited from confirmed old commits can remain by ID. Old format rejection and one-way layout transition are stated. The three cases above need normative rules, not only implementation advice or a future open point.\n\nSecurity: correctly distinguish unclear replies from explicit pack refusals and preserve canonical-writer fences. Do not infer retirement from one refused attempt while another may still write. Mandatory revocation/revert/policy activation must stay safely recorded under large collections. Admin-only failure attention and redacted remote details are appropriate. Unknown publication should also become visible after a defined delay, rather than remain silently pending as open point 40 acknowledges; this visibility can be added without abandoning its cohort.\n\nSimplification: one signed layout discriminator and deterministic boundaries are appropriate. B=8 MiB is a reasonable conservative transport choice against the measured 32 MiB provider limit, but it need not force a 1 MiB logical event cap. General bounded representations and deterministic tree fan-out avoid many special-case dropped-event rules. The optional checkpoint field and two attention variants are additive at the type level; serialization/verification changes still need the separate implementations.\n\nValidation: fresh npm ci (374 packages); root typecheck; UI tests 141 passed and CLI tests 102 passed. All six artifacts match the advertised head; all remaining source is byte-identical to main b5864882. I read the full amendment, types, reference example and UI/CLI changes. Four healthy boundary controls and the oversized-legacy-line diagnostic use the actual reference function, saved at /tmp/artroom-checker-log-Am4-diagnostics/layout.mjs. No source edits or live effects. The UI's blanket statement that publication retries after an hour or restart should distinguish unexpected-writer, for which the contract says pushing stops. Existing main's bounded-memory landing was also checked as a pure composition of the approved candidate and prior main: no novel files, no deletions and no overwritten later main edits. The implementation requests remain necessary after the contract review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:228c1e9fe9be3e52808696a76eaeef0b463fe9ef","binding":"assigned","head":"993e95d99e3645794e966b93abf969ffb1341db3","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3fb2812d0ffd16b728721c997359486b76bc7f91\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2f50fb7f7e1eda79085341fe5fd198d7f6c8e89c\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e4f7ef8c874f56dd74c6d672721e1dcd652acceb","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}